### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### studio-runs.http — the one door (ADR-068 §5)
### Was media-generation.http, which POSTed the three generation endpoints directly.
### Those endpoints are deleted: a capability is invoked by starting a RUN of the Studio
### that ships it, and there is no other way in. This contract therefore reads the
### composer row — what this deployment can launch from a single input — and starts a run
### from it, which is exactly what the chat bar's buttons do.
### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Authenticate
# @name login
POST {{$dotenv ROUTER_URL}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{$dotenv ADMIN_EMAIL}}",
  "password": "{{$dotenv ADMIN_PASSWORD}}"
}

?? status == 200

###

### The composer's button row — Studios launchable from one input
# Each entry carries what a client needs and nothing more: no uriPath, no httpMethod and
# no payloadTemplate, because the client no longer composes a call.
#
# Written in httpyac's own grammar, which is `?? [js] <selector> <operator> [value]` with the
# operator LAST. Two shapes were measured against it before this one was kept:
#
#   s.inputKind === "TEXT"              → SyntaxError: the `==` inside `===` is taken for the
#                                          operator and the expression reaches the evaluator as
#                                          `s.inputKind == = "TEXT"`.
#   ["TEXT","ASSET"].includes(s.inputKind) → NO ASSERTION AT ALL. No tick, no cross, the line simply
#                                          does not appear in the output. Worse than failing: an
#                                          assertion that silently does not run.
#
# So the predicate counts the entries that would be WRONG and compares that to zero, which puts the
# only `==` where httpyac expects it. Nothing here guards the next person; see ADR-069 §6.12.
# @name composer
GET {{$dotenv ROUTER_URL}}/api/v1/studios/composer?locale=fr
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{login.token}}

?? status == 200
?? js response.parsedBody.length > 0
?? js response.parsedBody.filter(s => !s.studioKey || !s.inputKey || !"TEXT ASSET".includes(s.inputKind)).length == 0
?? js response.parsedBody.filter(s => Object.hasOwn(s, "uriPath") || Object.hasOwn(s, "payloadTemplate")).length == 0

###

### Start a run of the first text-input Studio the row offers
# 202 with a run — the same acknowledgement a button gets. Accepted up to 409, which is how
# a Studio the account is not entitled to refuses (and carries the pack to open).
#
# `{{composer.$.[0].studioKey}}` was not valid JavaScript, so this request ERRORED on every run
# since it was written: httpyac evaluates `{{…}}` as an expression and `$.[0]` parses as nothing.
# It never reached the server once. A named request's parsed body is indexable directly, and the
# filter is what the heading above has always claimed this request does.
# @name startRun
POST {{$dotenv ROUTER_URL}}/api/v1/studios/{{composer.filter(s => s.inputKind == "TEXT")[0].studioKey}}/runs
Authorization: Bearer {{login.token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "inputs": {
    "{{composer.filter(s => s.inputKind == "TEXT")[0].inputKey}}": "A futuristic cityscape at sunset with neon lights"
  }
}

?? status >= 200
?? status < 500

###

### The run is readable, and it is the run that knows about the job it dispatched
# @name runDetail
GET {{$dotenv ROUTER_URL}}/api/v1/studios/runs/{{startRun.id}}
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{login.token}}

?? status >= 200
?? status < 500

###

### Starting a run unauthenticated is refused
# 401, or 403 where the availability gate answers first in the hermetic stack.
# @name runUnauth
POST {{$dotenv ROUTER_URL}}/api/v1/studios/image-generation/runs
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "inputs": { "prompt": "test" }
}

?? status >= 401
?? status < 404
